{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015oYEHibZV3uHYdcMfkUugq","type":"tool_result","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-8; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13, sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal, \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL percent-encoding, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML character entities. Every single_solve so far was ONE layer.\n- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 33, 38). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, in that order so far). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n- Clocks: TT about 5-8 s from day 2 on (30 s on day 1), mastery about 5-9 s (9 s on day 7, 8 s on day 8 with MORE sets: the window does not reliably grow with the set count). Only a deterministic solver survives these windows. The single_solve is untimed.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, `&#NNN;`\n  entities, ...); detect each line's encoding from its alphabet, not its position (position-based detection breaks if the order\n  changes). Rule for \"escape-token\" encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind\n  (`re.sub(token,'',frag).strip()==''`), then use the stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) so the unseen forms\n  (`%XX` upper, `&#x6d;` hex, `&amp;` named) are covered too. Day 8 GM bytes used only decimal `&#NNN;` in all 40 blobs.\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day (33 on day 7, 38 on day 8); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7 and 8). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621); same sentence and encoding.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| Magic `1f8b`/`78`/`BZh`/`fd377a58` | gunzip / zlib / bzip2 / xz (in the toolbox. Not seen in the GM bytes yet) |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Day 1: A Little Based** (\"sixty-four is a good base\").\n- Artifact: 148-byte base64, one layer, decoded with `base64 -d` -> slug. CORRECT on the first submit.\n- The first 6 worker launches died in under 8 s with \"OAuth session expired\" (harness auth, not the challenge). The successful worker took about 95 s.\n- TT and mastery windows were 30 s. Mastery: 3 sets, each blob ONE line `01\\t<base64>`.\n- Mastery failed about 12 times in a row. The cause was the HARNESS, not the decode: the contract listed 3 downloads all named `blob.txt`, and `hctf artifact` saved only set1 (see challenges/day01/warmups-a-little-based/HELP.md). The solver now matches files by the contract's `artifacts[].sha256`.\n\n**Day 2: Half a Turn** (\"Thirteen steps forward\").\n- 108-byte ROT13 text -> `tr` rot13 -> slug. CORRECT first submit. Worker about 124 s, mostly harness bookkeeping. One stale start.json, overwritten by a re-start with no attempt_id, broke the completion check. It was restored from log.jsonl.\n- TT window 5 s. Several TT submits came back `expired` before the solver became the fast path.\n- Mastery 8 sets: a single download, a ZIP named `blob-01.txt` holding `blob-01..08.txt`. Each blob is 2 lines: base64, then rot13, joined verbatim.\n\n**Day 3: Hex Appeal** (\"Sixteen ... fingers\").\n- Plain hex string -> `xxd -r -p`. The single_solve took seconds via the reused generic solver (no worker log).\n- Mastery 13 sets x 3 lines (b64 / rot13 / hex), window 7 s.\n- Live mastery TIMEOUT: `english()` counted single letters, so a hex line (\"a\", \"e\" heavy) scored as English and was never decoded. The BFS took about 32 s per blob. Fixes: score only words of 2+ letters, add the fast `strict_line` path (strict hex -> strict b64 -> best rot-N, about 0.2 s for 13 sets), and cap the peel BFS at 0.6 s per question.\n\n**Day 4: Two of a Kind** (\"only two sides\").\n- Space-separated 8-bit binary. Reused solver, seconds.\n- Mastery 18 sets x 4 lines (+ binary line), window 7 s. Try binary before hex: 8-bit 0/1 groups are also valid hex.\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:18:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants of the SAME day -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:50:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:67:## Codec table (`Codec_<id>='<rep>|<transform>|<key>'`, batch `rem HUNTRESS-CODEC:<rep>|<transform>`; seen D3-D8)\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:82:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:150:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:209:## Pitfalls that cost time\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:21:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:40:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:57:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:129:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:148:## Delivery layouts seen (resolve these before parsing)\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:160:## Pitfalls that cost time\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:11:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:23:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:41:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:91:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:97:## Pitfalls that cost time\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:24:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:41:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:59:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:116:## Day 8 — Swift Justice (Swift ELF, 67 MB)\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:130:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:162:## Pitfalls that cost time\nreference/techniques/mobile-android.md:23:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/mobile-android.md:35:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/mobile-android.md:68:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/mobile-android.md:143:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/mobile-android.md:157:## Pitfalls that cost time\nlib:\n__init__.py\n__pycache__\nandroid.py\nbinary.py\nciphers.py\ncodec.py\ncommon.py\ndecode.py\nelf.py\nghidra_scripts\nknowledge.py\nplaybook.py\npyc.py\nrefs\nscripts.py\nstego.py\nvocab.json\n\ntests:\n__pycache__\ndata\ntest_codec.py\ntest_day6_fixes.py\ntest_day7_fixes.py\ntest_day8_fixes.py\ntest_day9_prep.py\ntest_day9b.py\ntest_hardening.py\ntest_hb_budget.py\ntest_llm_failures.py\ntest_orchestrate.py\ntest_toolchain.py\ntest_variants.py\n032b8d0 Merge the rehearsal copy's Day 8 ratchet knowledge (main's own Day 8 ratchet had reverted)\ne6f556c binary D8 solver: absent recover_code symbol -> [] (stripped Mastery variant)\n016f767 reference: GM dual-pool API, Day 8 speed post-mortem, binary Day 8 notes","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1b2a5880-b146-4584-b0b5-a8899fa1bffe","uuid":"72fe5fa9-84df-47aa-98a8-d30aa32d7fe0","timestamp":"2026-10-09T13:02:23.969Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-8; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13, sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal, \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL percent-encoding, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML character entities. Every single_solve so far was ONE layer.\n- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 33, 38). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, in that order so far). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n- Clocks: TT about 5-8 s from day 2 on (30 s on day 1), mastery about 5-9 s (9 s on day 7, 8 s on day 8 with MORE sets: the window does not reliably grow with the set count). Only a deterministic solver survives these windows. The single_solve is untimed.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, `&#NNN;`\n  entities, ...); detect each line's encoding from its alphabet, not its position (position-based detection breaks if the order\n  changes). Rule for \"escape-token\" encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind\n  (`re.sub(token,'',frag).strip()==''`), then use the stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) so the unseen forms\n  (`%XX` upper, `&#x6d;` hex, `&amp;` named) are covered too. Day 8 GM bytes used only decimal `&#NNN;` in all 40 blobs.\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day (33 on day 7, 38 on day 8); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7 and 8). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621); same sentence and encoding.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| Magic `1f8b`/`78`/`BZh`/`fd377a58` | gunzip / zlib / bzip2 / xz (in the toolbox. Not seen in the GM bytes yet) |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Day 1: A Little Based** (\"sixty-four is a good base\").\n- Artifact: 148-byte base64, one layer, decoded with `base64 -d` -> slug. CORRECT on the first submit.\n- The first 6 worker launches died in under 8 s with \"OAuth session expired\" (harness auth, not the challenge). The successful worker took about 95 s.\n- TT and mastery windows were 30 s. Mastery: 3 sets, each blob ONE line `01\\t<base64>`.\n- Mastery failed about 12 times in a row. The cause was the HARNESS, not the decode: the contract listed 3 downloads all named `blob.txt`, and `hctf artifact` saved only set1 (see challenges/day01/warmups-a-little-based/HELP.md). The solver now matches files by the contract's `artifacts[].sha256`.\n\n**Day 2: Half a Turn** (\"Thirteen steps forward\").\n- 108-byte ROT13 text -> `tr` rot13 -> slug. CORRECT first submit. Worker about 124 s, mostly harness bookkeeping. One stale start.json, overwritten by a re-start with no attempt_id, broke the completion check. It was restored from log.jsonl.\n- TT window 5 s. Several TT submits came back `expired` before the solver became the fast path.\n- Mastery 8 sets: a single download, a ZIP named `blob-01.txt` holding `blob-01..08.txt`. Each blob is 2 lines: base64, then rot13, joined verbatim.\n\n**Day 3: Hex Appeal** (\"Sixteen ... fingers\").\n- Plain hex string -> `xxd -r -p`. The single_solve took seconds via the reused generic solver (no worker log).\n- Mastery 13 sets x 3 lines (b64 / rot13 / hex), window 7 s.\n- Live mastery TIMEOUT: `english()` counted single letters, so a hex line (\"a\", \"e\" heavy) scored as English and was never decoded. The BFS took about 32 s per blob. Fixes: score only words of 2+ letters, add the fast `strict_line` path (strict hex -> strict b64 -> best rot-N, about 0.2 s for 13 sets), and cap the peel BFS at 0.6 s per question.\n\n**Day 4: Two of a Kind** (\"only two sides\").\n- Space-separated 8-bit binary. Reused solver, seconds.\n- Mastery 18 sets x 4 lines (+ binary line), window 7 s. Try binary before hex: 8-bit 0/1 groups are also valid hex.\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:18:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants of the SAME day -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:50:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:67:## Codec table (`Codec_<id>='<rep>|<transform>|<key>'`, batch `rem HUNTRESS-CODEC:<rep>|<transform>`; seen D3-D8)\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:82:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:150:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/script-analysis.md:209:## Pitfalls that cost time\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:21:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:40:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:57:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:129:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:148:## Delivery layouts seen (resolve these before parsing)\nreference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md:160:## Pitfalls that cost time\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:11:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:23:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:41:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:91:## GM answer conventions learned\nreference/techniques/warmups.md:97:## Pitfalls that cost time\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:24:## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:41:## Recognition cues -> first move\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:59:## Day by day\nreference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md:116:## Day 8 — Swift Justice (Swift ELF, 67 MB)\nrefere